@ranking-behaviour
Feature: Ranking activities by weather suitability
  As a traveller comparing days,
  I want the ranking to reflect how suitable the weather is for each activity
  so that I can trust the recommendation without reading the raw forecast.

  The scoring model belongs to the developers and will be tuned, so these scenarios never pin
  exact scores. They pin behaviour any sensible model must show: obvious winners for clear-cut
  weather (decision table), scores that move in the right direction when one factor changes
  (metamorphic relations), and well-formed results for any combination of weather (pairwise).

  Rule: Clear-cut weather puts the obvious activity first

    @critical
    Scenario Outline: "<weather>" in <city> makes <best activity> the top-ranked activity
      Given the weather in "<city>" is "<weather>" all week
      When the user requests activity rankings for "<city>"
      Then "<best activity>" is ranked first on every day
      And "<best activity>" is rated at least "GOOD" on every day
      And "<unsuitable activity>" is rated "POOR" on every day

      Examples:
        | weather                 | city     | best activity       | unsuitable activity |
        | heavy snow and freezing | Chamonix | Skiing              | Surfing             |
        | clean surfing swell     | Biarritz | Surfing             | Skiing              |
        | clear and warm          | Lisbon   | Outdoor Sightseeing | Skiing              |
        | storm with heavy rain   | Biarritz | Indoor Sightseeing  | Surfing             |

  Rule: Each day is ranked on its own weather

    Scenario: A single snowy day among mild days changes only that day's ranking
      Given the weather in "Chamonix" is "mild and overcast" all week
      And the weather in "Chamonix" on day 3 is "heavy snow and freezing"
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Chamonix"
      Then "Skiing" is ranked first on day 3
      And "Skiing" is not ranked first on any day except day 3

    Scenario: Identical weather on every day produces identical assessments
      Rankings must be deterministic: the same conditions cannot score differently
      just because they fall on another date.

      Given the weather in "Biarritz" is "clean surfing swell" all week
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Biarritz"
      Then every day has the same scores, ratings and ranks

  Rule: Changing one weather factor moves scores in the expected direction

    Scenario Outline: A <factor> of <changed value> leaves the <activity> score <relation> the unchanged day
      Day 1 keeps the baseline weather and day 2 differs only by the named factor,
      so any score difference between the two days is caused by that factor alone.

      Given the weather in "<city>" is "<baseline weather>" all week
      And day 2 in "<city>" differs only by a <factor> of "<changed value>"
      When the user requests activity rankings for "<city>"
      Then the "<activity>" score on day 2 is <relation> the score on day 1

      Examples:
        | city     | baseline weather        | factor              | changed value | activity            | relation    |
        | Chamonix | heavy snow and freezing | snowfall            | 60 cm         | Skiing              | at least    |
        | Chamonix | heavy snow and freezing | maximum temperature | 14 °C         | Skiing              | at most     |
        | Lisbon   | clear and warm          | rain                | 25 mm         | Outdoor Sightseeing | at most     |
        | Lisbon   | clear and warm          | rain                | 25 mm         | Indoor Sightseeing  | at least    |
        | Lisbon   | clear and warm          | wind speed          | 70 km/h       | Outdoor Sightseeing | at most     |
        | Biarritz | clean surfing swell     | wave height         | 0.2 m         | Surfing             | at most     |
        | Biarritz | clean surfing swell     | wave height         | 7 m           | Surfing             | at most     |

  Rule: Any combination of weather produces a well-formed ranking
    The three weeks below are generated by `npm run pairwise:generate`: across them, every
    pair of levels of any two weather factors occurs on at least one day, covering all 166
    pairs in 20 days instead of 1,296 full combinations. Unlikely mixes such as hot weather
    with heavy snow are deliberate: the API must stay well-formed whatever the provider returns.
    Days a week does not list keep unremarkable weather.

    Scenario: Pairwise week 1 of 3 is ranked without gaps or invalid values
      Given the weather in "Biarritz" day by day is:
        | day | temperature | snowfall | rain  | wind   | waves          | sky          |
        | 1   | freezing    | none     | none  | calm   | no marine data | clear        |
        | 2   | freezing    | light    | light | breezy | flat           | overcast     |
        | 3   | freezing    | heavy    | heavy | gale   | surfable       | thunderstorm |
        | 4   | cold        | none     | light | gale   | dangerous      | clear        |
        | 5   | cold        | light    | none  | calm   | surfable       | overcast     |
        | 6   | cold        | heavy    | none  | breezy | no marine data | thunderstorm |
        | 7   | mild        | none     | heavy | breezy | flat           | clear        |
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Biarritz"
      Then the response satisfies the activity ranking contract
      And every day lists Skiing, Surfing, Outdoor Sightseeing and Indoor Sightseeing exactly once
      And every day ranks its activities 1 to 4 without ties
      And every day ranks a higher-scoring activity above a lower-scoring one
      And every assessed activity has a score between 0 and 100
      And every activity has at least one reason

