  Rule: Extreme but real weather is handled at the boundaries

    Scenario Outline: "<weather>" still produces valid assessments
      Given the weather in "Chamonix" is "<weather>" all week
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Chamonix"
      Then the response satisfies the activity ranking contract
      And every assessed activity has a score between 0 and 100

      Examples:
        | weather         |
        | extreme heat    |
        | extreme cold    |
        | record snowfall |

  Rule: Missing data is reported, never invented

    @critical
    Scenario: A day without forecast values is marked unavailable instead of guessed
      Given the weather in "Chamonix" is "clear and warm" all week
      And the weather provider has no data for "Chamonix" on day 5
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Chamonix"
      Then every activity on day 5 is rated "UNAVAILABLE" without a score
      And every reason on day 5 explains that forecast data is missing
      And no activity is rated "UNAVAILABLE" on any day except day 5

    Scenario: Surfing at an inland city is rated poor because there is no sea
      Open-Meteo's marine API answers inland coordinates with null wave values, not an error.

      Given the weather in "Madrid" is "clear and warm" all week
      When the user requests activity rankings for "Madrid"
      Then "Surfing" is rated "POOR" on every day
      And "Surfing" is ranked last on every day
      And the reasons for "Surfing" explain that there is no surf forecast for this location
